WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.030
People try to say, well, you
should try to learn 10 words a day

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.300
and really make sure you can use
them and sort of nail them down.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.490
I've never found that effective.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.470
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.440
Today I wanna talk about learning
vocabulary and why we shouldn't

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.450
try to remember vocabulary.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.900
Now, if you enjoy my videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.100
the be for notification.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.850
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.365
I mentioned the other day that
I went to visit my brother who's

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:33.015
not well, he's sort of bedridden.

00:00:33.315 --> 00:00:36.455
I gave him an iPad and I tried
to get him going on LingQ.

00:00:36.644 --> 00:00:39.675
So it's very interesting for me,
and I speak to him every couple of

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.985
days, it's very interesting for me
to see his experience because he's

00:00:45.015 --> 00:00:47.444
not an experienced language learner.

00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:52.485
And so he's learning LingQ and
he's getting used to learning

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:54.345
in his case Spanish using LingQ.

00:00:55.004 --> 00:00:59.585
And so he said to me on several
occasions, I keep on trying to remember

00:01:00.165 --> 00:01:04.185
this sentence or this word, and I can't
remember it, then it's quite frustrating.

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:09.735
And so I try to explain to him
again that, you know, don't

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:11.745
worry about not remembering.

00:01:11.745 --> 00:01:16.365
In fact, don't even try to remember
the vocabulary, keep moving,

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:20.805
keep moving on and eventually
the words will start to stick.

00:01:21.945 --> 00:01:26.940
And I thought of my own process
of language learning and, I mean,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.510
the bulk of my words I learn them
through listening and reading.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.750
Um, I look words up every time.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.050
Like if I'm on my lesson page in
LingQ, if it's a blue word, obviously

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.230
I look it up in order to get the
meaning, it's now a yellow word.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.640
But when I come across a yellow word, even
if I kind of think I know the meaning,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.070
I'll just give another quick look.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.190
Uh, and then of course, that
doesn't mean I can use it.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.610
It doesn't mean that I won't, again, have
to look it up the next time, but it's

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.445
part of that gradual process of getting a
sense of what the scope of meaning of the

00:01:59.445 --> 00:02:04.395
word is as I see it in different contexts,
getting familiar with the word, the word

00:02:04.395 --> 00:02:09.615
getting familiar with me, and, and at some
point it starts to click in and it's very

00:02:09.615 --> 00:02:11.355
hard to predict when that's gonna happen.

00:02:12.135 --> 00:02:16.235
And so most of my activity is listening
and reading, noticing things while

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.095
reading, noticing things while listening
but I also do review words in list.

00:02:23.279 --> 00:02:27.359
Uh, and I've said that I'll sometimes
pick, you know, the status three, the sort

00:02:27.359 --> 00:02:33.269
of familiar words to try and move some
to known to pad my known words number.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.950
Uh, I'll sometimes look at them
in alphabetical order just to see

00:02:37.954 --> 00:02:41.820
the words that have the same, you
know, prefix, because that can help.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:47.700
Uh, if I do flashcards, I want all the
information on the face of the flashcard.

00:02:48.029 --> 00:02:49.989
It's all just exposure.

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:53.355
I don't try to rack my brain.

00:02:53.535 --> 00:02:55.245
In other words, have the...

00:02:55.335 --> 00:02:58.785
either the word in the target language
on the front of the flashcard, and then

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:04.095
try to think what it means and flip
it over, or conversely to see, say the

00:03:04.095 --> 00:03:07.905
English word and try to remember what
that is, uh, in the target language.

00:03:07.905 --> 00:03:08.985
I don't do those things.

00:03:09.225 --> 00:03:09.945
Some people do.

00:03:09.945 --> 00:03:13.065
I'm not saying that it's not
effective for some people, but

00:03:13.065 --> 00:03:14.895
for me, I don't believe in it.

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:20.910
I, I, I feel that if I can continue
to expose myself or expose these

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.320
words to my brain in different
ways eventually I will learn them.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:31.320
And I have noticed that a lot of the words
that I learned that are added to my known

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.990
words total are words that I didn't even
look up because words, you know, they

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:42.540
have things in common and pretty soon
we know, uh, maybe a different tense or

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.630
a different person or a different form
of a word that we've already learned.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:51.450
And so without even looking that word
up, it's just added to our known words

00:03:51.455 --> 00:03:54.900
total because we didn't save it and
so the system considers it known.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:04:00.630
So I sometimes feel that this sort of
deliberate attempt to acquire words

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.320
and what's particularly bad is, is,
is if people try to say, well, you

00:04:04.325 --> 00:04:07.770
should try to learn 10 words a day
and really make sure you can use

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.570
them and sort of nail them down.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.710
I've never found that effective.

00:04:12.225 --> 00:04:18.135
I far rather just expose myself to
as many words as I can rereading old

00:04:18.135 --> 00:04:23.865
lessons, you know, going into new lessons,
exposing myself to these words and

00:04:23.865 --> 00:04:29.415
gradually low and behold the statistics
in increase and my known words total

00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:31.365
all of a sudden it's 10,000 words.

00:04:31.665 --> 00:04:36.645
Granted, the way we count them at LingQ
where each form of the word is considered

00:04:36.645 --> 00:04:44.580
a different word, but the point is
don't in my view, don't rely on memory.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.890
Like a lot of people say to me,
well, I can't learn languages

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.230
because, uh, I have a poor memory.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.430
Your poor memory is probably
not that poor, it's irrelevant.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.550
Language learning is
not about memorization.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.580
Language learning is about
committing yourself to a

00:04:59.585 --> 00:05:01.700
process, enjoying the process.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:06.420
Allowing the language to come into
you and trusting that your brain will

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.190
gradually understand more and more,
gradually acquire more and more words.

00:05:11.195 --> 00:05:16.099
So my advice on language learning, or
in, in terms of acquiring words, and I

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:21.330
should say, accumulating words is the
fundamental task in language learning.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:25.289
And so the more words you have
even passively the better your

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:26.400
potential in the language.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.520
So it is important to acquire the
words, but you're not necessarily

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.419
gonna acquire words by deliberately
trying to remember them.

00:05:34.229 --> 00:05:37.590
Uh, a couple of videos I'll leave you with
one where I talk about learning a hundred

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:43.530
words a day and another one where I talk
about memorization and language learning.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.179
I did these a few years ago.

00:05:45.585 --> 00:05:49.215
Hopefully I said the same
things then as I'm saying now.

00:05:49.695 --> 00:05:50.605
Thank you for listening.

00:05:50.745 --> 00:05:51.245
Bye for now.

